#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125513} :activity/id #uuid "665ce843-c897-5cef-9141-25ecb7cdc260" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050456 :topic/id #uuid "4a81b34b-9d6c-50ec-87c8-f6c706602e63" :topic/math-academy-id 1894} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125828 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadric surface\n\n$$\nz^2 = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\nThis is an example of an **elliptic cone**. It has its **vertex** at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the cone.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic cone whose vertex is at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(z-z_0)^2 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants." :tutorial/id #uuid "bf9fab37-b71c-5d84-95d5-791cd41706a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4924 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8afa0f97-79d7-5556-8054-cf7a927fbc25" :step/math-academy-id 30715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125514}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124585 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124396 :question/id #uuid "c351fdde-ce68-5fa1-97e1-7678211a3963" :question/math-academy-id "e-8216"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e0b4012-403b-5739-8f33-3c79111129fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051081}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520496}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Elliptic Cones"} :step/id #uuid "b83d2704-1cd8-5d3e-a5d6-3347b6842a7c" :step/math-academy-id 30716 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124586 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124397 :question/id #uuid "89ae8962-7ad4-5860-b070-8df4d92128ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8217"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb7148a2-58cc-5023-845c-435b47e31c83" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571441}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Elliptic Cones"} :step/id #uuid "60b53775-0c8d-50d6-8793-5131aef5e1ab" :step/math-academy-id 30717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125829 :tutorial/content "So far, we've encountered cones whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. \nLet's now consider the cases when a cone is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor an elliptic cone centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the $y^2$ term is isolated on one side, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis.\n\n$$\ny^2 = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/06/06c02be2e3ee96862abacc19885e34a8b3cb096a1b85417169022394839e7888.png)\n\n- When the $x^2$ term is isolated on one side, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis.\n\n$$\nx^2 = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/0e/0ef83c7e023ddcf5e408c7b8cb4e97deb7ece2f366628e019aa04ef90810c6f9.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3ba615cd-207c-53f0-ac45-57c3b3f46b84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4925 :tutorial/title "Alternative Orientations of the Axis of Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "63150029-88d0-527b-9d21-8af46b91197c" :step/math-academy-id 30720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125882 :tutorial/content "The traces of an elliptic cone are either intersecting lines, ellipses, hyperbolas, or points.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the elliptic cone given by\n\n$$\nz^2 = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/ba/ba552154b10ae7919496031613005c60461272923c6cae262a757b6df89c398d.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/2e/2e22ee99e93475eabae9ce8caacee814d0066dd30042367668542485cdacd8a3.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=k$ for $k \\neq 0$ is an ellipse:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = k^2\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d32e0c05b0bd5a55ff38183b362eebd44bf668dc9ee68ec5b6ae2274fdae934e.png)\n\n- The traces of our surface with the plane $x=k$ or $y=k$ for $k \\neq 0$ are hyperbolas:\n\n$$\nz^2-y^2 = k^2 \\qquad \\textrm{or}\\qquad z^2-x^2 = k^2\n$$\n\n![](images/23/23dd8112391406afd1da714df32d3a01ddb99b68c3b0885078ad76ebba23f9b5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "efd10d7e-d93a-5f24-b896-67f70dc4aa16" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7841 :tutorial/title "Traces of Elliptic Cones"} :step/id #uuid "b8cf188e-213a-5603-a9b9-fc97304c8d5c" :step/math-academy-id 45285 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125516}}] :activity/title "Elliptic Cones" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124697}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416617} :activity/id #uuid "22b10c8c-4354-5ffb-badf-062a6db3301e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050456 :topic/id #uuid "4a81b34b-9d6c-50ec-87c8-f6c706602e63" :topic/math-academy-id 1894} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045932cb-4e7d-5eeb-a1bd-fd8a8b320228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416608} {:answer/id #uuid "3565bdc3-f252-59de-885d-0bb85488a4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8,12,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416609} {:answer/id #uuid "57c975a4-5dc8-50d2-a81e-558a7189251f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-6,-4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416610} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea0ec23-f682-53e7-88fb-7469662cfac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416611} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc3119d-6815-5c0c-a167-9b6d25d35a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416611} :answer-field/id #uuid "90f98d7f-bc9e-5d73-8a9b-c9601cd09a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e365d16e-a317-5782-a1fd-f9a127eeaeee" :question/math-academy-id "q-94699" :question/problem "Find the center of the elliptic cone $8(x-6)^{2}+12(y-4)^{2}=4(z+2)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(x-6)^{2}+12(y-4)^{2} & =4(z+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{1/8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{1/12} & =\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{1/4} \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{1/8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{1/12} & =\\frac{(z-(-2))^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(6,4,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "5a0f177f-5462-56d0-97ab-220aea3f5556"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "497c9287-8527-5f28-9070-29d5455ff937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355315} {:answer/id #uuid "8935fc20-662f-5493-8895-2ff5e7873ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm 3x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355877} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd8d2b3-1b54-51b9-aa4b-86e2141de38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{x}{3}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355907} {:answer/id #uuid "97966c4c-9e1f-5c8e-b01d-facfe4e419d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9,2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356000} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f64f22-7913-52f4-86c8-f51e90ac0c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{2x}{3}+18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355315} :answer-field/id #uuid "8196235b-25ca-50fc-919f-d7ee8e52fb0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2cfd332-5d5c-5209-92ee-4f9962118084" :question/math-academy-id "q-94496" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cone $9(x-2)^{2}+2{y}^{2}=18{z}^{2}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x-2)^{2}+2{y}^{2}=18(0)^{2} \\\\ 9(x-2)^{2}+2{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives in the $xy$-plane the point $(2,0,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "81a2407c-e479-5098-9a3a-1ffa4cdd92c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096807f4-6104-5c9d-9a4f-3cc078997926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{y}{3}\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354752} {:answer/id #uuid "45b089cf-4cd4-5955-98fe-9f0ff803df9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=\\pm \\frac{y}{9}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355284} {:answer/id #uuid "78a27687-7772-5319-a40c-aef31e7c2570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=\\pm \\frac{y}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355718} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd6a87d-0609-5bbe-9ab6-30cf1a7bcfa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{y}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355784} {:answer/id #uuid "97dcd38b-595a-5837-97a8-ed1062f9f32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=\\pm \\frac{y}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355718} :answer-field/id #uuid "0000aeb3-77f2-52e8-8fd1-917fe7c0e597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1057b215-35c6-5a51-b1d3-2145bec593cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94486" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cone $\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}={z}^{2}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\frac{y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two lines in the $yz$-plane with equations $z=\\pm \\frac{y}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d217a96c-8c03-502b-b5e2-7536101a5ec4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416616}}] :activity/title "Elliptic Cones" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416607}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520571} :activity/id #uuid "b9323fcd-e255-54f9-952c-315c707f7555" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050456 :topic/id #uuid "4a81b34b-9d6c-50ec-87c8-f6c706602e63" :topic/math-academy-id 1894} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce436d1-a9c1-525b-a0b3-0ec1173836fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=\\pm \\frac{1}{4}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358002} {:answer/id #uuid "4856d8fc-d720-5b06-83ea-aae77c408c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358371} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad2e3a4-9385-5c7f-9bd1-08c990264005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=\\pm 4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358392} {:answer/id #uuid "af8462af-f75c-5645-b5c3-7168a1d4b045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=-4x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359278} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4fad8-ff94-5047-96b6-9810538bd050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{1}{4}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358392} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1f85999-f4fb-5e4b-82da-6df883efe9f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d65c16f-ccd9-5975-b9f2-fd4e5517583b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95462" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the elliptic cone $(x-1)^{2}+7{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{16}{z}^{2}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+7(0)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{z}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}z & =\\pm (x-1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 4(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two lines in the $xz$-plane with equations $z=\\pm 4(x-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "4d5600cf-e69e-5c6c-99e5-f393d1738070" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61541d10-8686-53a1-b1bf-94e0ef8661d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398626} {:answer/id #uuid "57cf4f60-5a5b-5023-8efc-4c4539d402c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398627} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec42430-265d-5e60-ab37-b8ed51e07c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398628} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb43111-b5c6-506a-9572-f434c68db87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398629} {:answer/id #uuid "7a27a7e6-a9f2-5d69-9ce2-25ac6a1448c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398630}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398629} :answer-field/id #uuid "98a42fbd-b4c4-5033-b650-f9ced006793a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ba92e56-0c67-534f-87e7-bf0cdfa829cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-176834" :question/problem "Find the center of the elliptic cone $4(x-3)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2}=20(z-1)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2} & =20(z-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/5} & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{1/20} \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/5} & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{1/20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(3,-2,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "618882c4-39c0-5422-a1a5-8424f45a62bc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e63f99-8f14-5080-9a2e-a03f67b6f5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,1,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319604} {:answer/id #uuid "682d8bcb-bb0a-5b03-84fe-94c34cbeb032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(5,0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319711} {:answer/id #uuid "9f857228-b831-5b0a-ae8c-f7da0874af6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319867} {:answer/id #uuid "a6841569-84af-5be4-9d81-2ecad2a172f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}+1,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319888} {:answer/id #uuid "b27e7f2e-0e94-55d0-b201-8607ca8c4da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319867} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ec7c853-e0da-5ec1-be58-35c4e6defe4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "986a46a0-87d4-5e95-a284-ff13a2e0b6b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-35266" :question/problem "Given that the trace of the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}={z}^{2}$ and the $xz$-plane are two lines, what is the intersection point of these lines?" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\pm \\frac{x-1}{2} & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two lines in the $xz$-plane with equations $z=\\frac{x-1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{1-x}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the point of intersection of both lines, we equate the right-hand sides. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\frac{1-x}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =1-x \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ in the equation of the first line and solving for $z,$ we obtain $z=\\frac{1-1}{2}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(1,0,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "7489fe9f-b6a1-5b05-8890-f8dbae2bb299" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149d7ad3-6a01-5174-82bf-5d60b9244ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,0,0)$ and $(8,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520018} {:answer/id #uuid "200ae72a-6343-55a0-bb64-42d0cfc2e032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,0,0)$ and $(10,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520036} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdd0c2a-eed2-5368-b2da-6b29b25677d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(4,0,0)$ and $(10,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520170} {:answer/id #uuid "c850056d-98c5-53e9-817e-b69ff41dd285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520271} {:answer/id #uuid "eee23346-be71-5a2e-883c-63ac858b2a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(4,0,0)$ and $(8,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520343} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b271f10-b638-58d9-8c15-6f54a2760bc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c06702a9-7f9f-5ebf-bbd9-2e359cfd7640" :question/math-academy-id "q-94606" :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8}=(z-2)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{8} & =(0-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{16}{8} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+2 & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-6 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =6\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =4,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(4,0,0)$ and $(8,0,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "7d8bace2-f443-5b72-a65f-94700f3f5957" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431915b2-15e4-559a-9ff5-a1abef76b999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355262} {:answer/id #uuid "69b4bd2c-81f2-5ad5-b932-fc660accbada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355596} {:answer/id #uuid "976223ef-b049-5a60-8175-d7ccdf55f16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355995} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cb07b7-3acf-5d59-ba6d-f563d82a166a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356150} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3da815-6277-5b7a-9d14-81d8eb8cfaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356718} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2daa040-b350-5a0f-89d7-1814b47a133e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6dbbc8b0-b994-5e7b-854a-3bf51bb1a258" :question/math-academy-id "q-94082" :question/problem "Find the center of the elliptic cone $2(x+2)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}={z}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{1/3} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{1/3} & =(z-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(-2,1,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "86ddb20a-1f36-5b9e-8a19-40c4fef5a0e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520576}}] :activity/title "Elliptic Cones" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519994}]}
